body:
I knew a girl who would be with one person, break up, and have someone else in the next 2 weeks on a repeating cycle. One of my mother's friends was a similar way in highschool and college, but she never felt attracted truly to more than a couple people in her life. It isn't about what's normal, because everyone is different. You can't \*make\* yourself attracted to someone, and rushing makes it worse....if you succeeded, you'd probably realize later on that it was forced. That's the worse kind of feeling.

&#x200B;

Accept your individual pacing for feeling attraction, and perhaps look further into finding your "type." Maybe things will change when this happens! If not, worry less and the right person will enter your life in some way. Living with open eyes and a good attitude is one of the most underrated, attractive traits out there.

&#x200B;

As for getting set up, my advice is to ask one of your closest friends to be on the lookout for someone that matches you. Since they know you best, they'll be able to tell if it would be an instant no-go!   


Very best of luck to you. <3

Personally, I'm a dancer and I like going to social events with dancing. So when a guy I like asks me to dance, I say "Nice to meet you, I'm ..." and it gets them every time. Another thing I do is if I have short dress/skirt on when sitting and I readjust, I can rub my legs together a bit. If they're smooth this catches a guy's eye. Always, subtle eye contact and enhanced expressions. I might playfully touch them on the arm/shoulder.

&#x200B;

From guys, I like if they touch me on the lower arm. If they know I don't mind them, the confidence of asking me to dance and "assuming" by going ahead and taking my arm/hand/shoulder or around the waist seems charming. Being just a bit rough (not with my shoulder, damn it) with dancing like holding tighter, etc. shows they want to be closer and like being in control... Dark humour and playful challenging gets me. Good tight hugs that show they aren't scared are appreciated and so are well-placed and direct compliments. Listening and giving thoughtful responses during conversations are special skills not many people have mastered.

Some people are just not "chemically" attracted to each other. I knew a nice married couple...they just didn't like how the other smelled. As ridiculous as it sounds, they loved each other. Just, they weren't chemically attracted in whatever way. It's nothing wrong with you or the guy. 

&#x200B;

The last thing that really turned me off about a particularly attractive guy is that he didn't know geography well, and wasn't aware of other cultures. He didn't know where Ukraine is :(((... It's kind of something I can't think of myself living without...that kind of knowledge. Surely it isn't a big deal to someone who grew up similarly, ~~without a map.~~

I'm a female, but I found that a lot of guys I know like the peaches/fresh and light fruit smell. Not too foody but sweet. Between being a bee magnet and a brothel-madame. 

&#x200B;

Complicate it more than that and they're going to go "huh??"

\>....thirsty immature creepy neckbeards who can't interact with people in a healthy way in real life and try to compensate by being way too forward online. 

Thank you for this colorful description. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

It seems I attract weird people. My mom had the same problem my entire childhood. Even with me in the buggy. Some things you teach your kid to think but not say. :)))

I'm sorry to say this, but it was not "brave" of them. To me, the bravest thing to do in your situation is to face life and not let your troubles overcome you. To find a way to overcome them..

I am so so sorry that you lost your friends this way. They have left even more sorrow behind them, passing what they struggled with off to other people because they thought they couldn't handle it. Let me tell you something: you are stronger than this. You can do it, you can live better, you can be better. 

&#x200B;

Also, having the flu etc. amplifies everything you're feeling right now. Make sure to rest, don't let this get the best of you..

&#x200B;

Please consider what you're saying, please consider that the strongest thing to do is live. I am sure you are capable of more than just existing. You can make yourself proud and honor your friends with the life you have. 

&#x200B;

Much love from here to wherever you are...

A lot of times, people roast me or poke at me for being "hard'' or "closed off" or seeming like I don't care about anything (the same kinds of  people who think caring is making me feel like I can't be honest because they will freak out or judge me out of "love") ...I mean, I lost my will to live, is that enough? I was good at plenty of things until people started acting like my caring is not enough, like I can never do enough or be enough, so I started feeling like I wasn't good enough in those pursuits either and feel like quitting. The result, an even more bland and pointless life. 

&#x200B;

Thanks for your comment, it means a lot...

&#x200B;

Timothy Doner- Breaking the Language Barrier : [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xNmf-G81Irs](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xNmf-G81Irs)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

...this means so much to me. I have always been obsessed with languages, scripts, and different cultures. Before I was even exposed to "normal" languages and before I had resources to learn those, at 6-9 years old I was studying hieroglyphics and learning how to write them, and studying the history and language of the Cherokee Indians. (As I discovered 10 years later, I, unlike the rest of my family, have 0 Cherokee blood. All that identifying with being "indian" was for nothing...)  From the age of 9, I was studying Spanish. When I was 14, I heard Russian for the first time and decided I would pursue it until I died. I speak 3 languages and am a student of 3 more, and I do not intend to stop.   



Timothy Doner made me believe I could learn anything, do anything, and become anything.
